cat
-------
cat--> display the conentet of the file.
	-cat filename.txt
cat -b filename.txt ---- add the line num to non blank lines.
cat > filename.txt  ---- '>' this redirects and open the file for edit.
cat -n filename.txt ---- add the line num to all the lines.
cat >> filename.txt  --- '>>' this redirects and edit without overwriting the previous date of the file.
cat file1 > file2 ---- this redirects the contents of file1 to file2.
---------------
copy
------
cp --->{source file to destination file}
cp sum1 sum2 -----> sum1 is copied in sum2
cp -r dir1 dir2 ---> copy dir1 inside dir2
cp -r dir1 new_dir ---> i will create a new dir and copy inside that dir.
cp -ri dir1 dir2 --->it will ask permisiion to overwrite or not.  

---------
move
---------
mv source file destination file.
mv dir1 dir2
mv jink jink2 ----> if jink2 is not exist then it will create it and mv data

note:
1) mv command we can give single source file to single destination file.
2) we can move multiple files to single directory (but the dir should exist)
3) we can move single dir to single dir (its not compulsary that destination dir sould exists.
4) we can move multiple dir to single dir (but destination dir should exist).


meta character/wildcard character

it is used in file regular expressions.
it is used for pattern detection.

* --->it will show all the file /dir
?---->signle character
[] ---> anything in betwwn the range [a-z] [A-Z][0-9]
\ ----> ignore the special meaning 
		ex: \? 
{} --->it is used for multiple patterns, this characters used in regular expressions for pttern matching.

examples:
1) list out file which is haveing name 3 charcaters only but end with at least 2 numbers with any extention.
 ls [a-z][0-9][0-9].*
 		or
 ls ?[0-9][0-9].*
 
 2) list out all the names which starts with d and ends with s with any extention?
		ls d*s.*
3) list all the names with 2 characters with .txt extention ?
	ls ??.txt
4) list out all the names which start with a or m or c with any extention?
	ls [amc]*.*

5) list out all the names with lower case with any extention?
	ls [a-z]*.*

