File compare commands:

cmp: ---> compare two files byte by byte.
diff: ---> allows you to compare two files line by line. 

wc:---->used to find out number of lines, words count, byte and characters count in the files specified in the file arguments.
	wc -l: ---> print no of lines(wc filename)
	wc -w: ---> print no of words
	wc -c: ---> print no of bytes.

comm: --->compare two sorted files line by line

		ex: comm m.txt m1.txt 
	comm m.txt m1.txt
                a
                b
                c
        E
	comm: file 2 is not in sorted order
	f
	comm: input is not in sorted order
note: then save the sorted file and execute.
		sort m1.txt -o m1.txt
		comm m.txt m1.txt


	
sort: ---> sort lines of text files.

find
--------
Find :---->it is used to search files and dir recursively.
	-name
	-type
	-time
	-permissions

find [path] [options] [actions]

path --> where to search
options ---> conditions ( name, type)
actions ---> what to do (print, delete, execute)

 	find ./ -type d -empty ---> to get empty dir
	find ./ -type f -empty  ----> to get empty files.
	find ./ "*" -type f ---> extract the files in the current dir.
	find ./ "*" -type d  ----> extact the dir.
	find ./ -name "x2.tcl" -type f  --> whether file is there or not.
	find /cygdrive/c/Users/mogut/OneDrive/Desktop/* -type f------> getting all the file present inside that dir by using path.
 find . -type f -mtime +1 -mtime -3  ----> it will extract files created in between 1-3 days.
find . -type f -ctime +1
find . -type f -mmin +1
	'-n' -->less than n (recent)
	'+n' --> more than n (older)
	'n'  --> extacly n 

 --
 finding empty files in current dir
 	
find ./ -type f -empty
	find ./ -type f -size 0

finding the files by permiossions
	find ./ -perm 777



	---------------------------

	piping

	--> Using piping multiple comamands works together.
	--> we can use piping vertical bar ( | )

	ex: command1 --> output ---> command2 ---> final output


	xargs ---> its used to give as a standard input/ arguments to the commands .

			seq 100 | xargs -i mkdir {}
					---> creates 100 empty dir.
			seq 100 | xargs -i rmdir {}
					---> remove all empty files.

			seq 100 | head -10 | xargs -i rmdir {}
			----> to remove 1-10 dir.
                        ls | wc -l filename
                        ls ----> show the files
                        |-----> pipe(input-output | input output)
                        wc ----> word count
                        -l ----> show lines


	tee command:
	In between the pipeing if we use '>' symbol it will break the pipeing.

		example: ls -l > output | wc

	use tee | tee concept
		ls -l | tee |wc
	example:
 		ls -l |tee file1.txt | wc file1.txt
                ls -l |tee file1.txt | tee file2.txt | wc -l
	
